INT. BOWLING ALLEY - AFTER HOURS - NIGHT

Dead neon. Sixteen lanes. The smell of old shoe spray and fryer oil.

A banner over the counter, curling at one corner: LUCKY STRIKE LANES - SINCE 1974. Beneath it, a handwritten sign: CLOSED FOR GOOD.

Cardboard boxes line the shoe counter. A radio plays low, a ballgame, nobody listening.

ROSALIND OKONKWO-HART (40s, tired eyes, her late father's cardigan two sizes too big) wraps trophies in newspaper. Methodical. Careful. Every one gets its own page.

Her brother DEVANTE (30s, leather jacket, restless hands) slouches against lane nine, flicking the lever on the ball return. Click. Click. Click.

Between them, on a stool: MRS. PEARL ADEYEMI (70s, church hat, a purse held like a shield). Their father's oldest friend. She hasn't taken her coat off.

Click. Click.

ROSALIND
Stop that.

DEVANTE
It's a lever.

ROSALIND
It's loud.

He stops. Picks at a splinter on the rail. Rosalind turns a trophy over in her hands. A bowler, gold paint chipped off one knee.

PEARL
He bowled a 290 once.

ROSALIND
I know.

PEARL
Nineteen eighty-eight.

DEVANTE
It was a 289.

PEARL
He told me 290.

DEVANTE
He rounded up.

PEARL
He did not round.

Rosalind sets the trophy in a box. Takes another. Wraps. The paper crackles.

DEVANTE
(to Rosalind)
You're keeping those?

ROSALIND
They're his.

DEVANTE
They're junk.

ROSALIND
Then leave them.

DEVANTE
I'm not leaving anything. I'm just asking.

He kicks a bowling ball. It rolls an inch, rocks back. He stares at it.

PEARL
The buyer's coming at nine?

ROSALIND
Eight.

PEARL
He's turning it into a gym.

ROSALIND
I know.

PEARL
A gym.

DEVANTE
Pearl.

PEARL
I'm only saying.

Devante crosses to the pin deck. Climbs over the gutter rail, and walks down lane nine toward the pins. Rosalind looks up. Pearl looks up. He walks the entire length, boots loud on the wood, and stops at the foul line, back to them.

ROSALIND
Don't scuff the boards.

DEVANTE
They're getting torn out.

ROSALIND
Still.

He steps off the lane. Picks up a ball. Weighs it. Sets it down.

Rosalind tapes a box shut. The tape shrieks. Pearl flinches.

PEARL
He always said you'd be the one to close up.

Rosalind's hands stop. Then keep going.

ROSALIND
I'm not closing anything. The bank is.

PEARL
You know what I mean.

ROSALIND
I don't.

Devante turns. Pearl turns. Rosalind lifts the box, struggling with its weight. Devante and Pearl both step forward, then both freeze. Rosalind sets the box down herself.

DEVANTE
Let me.

ROSALIND
I've got it.

DEVANTE
You'll hurt your back.

ROSALIND
I've hurt it before.

She lifts. Carries it to the door. Sets it on the step. Cold air floods the lanes.

PEARL
Rosalind, honey, your coat.

ROSALIND
I'm fine.

PEARL
You're shaking.

ROSALIND
It's cold.

Pearl reaches into her purse. Pulls out a folded bowling shirt, faded green, a name embroidered on the pocket: CHUKS.

She holds it out. Rosalind doesn't take it.

DEVANTE
(quiet)
Where'd you get that?

PEARL
He left it in my car. Years ago.

Silence. The radio crackles: a crowd roars somewhere far away.

Devante takes the shirt. Looks at it. Folds it once more, carefully, along the same creases. Walks to the box by the door. Lays it on top.

He doesn't say anything. Neither does Rosalind. She tapes the flaps shut.

Pearl finally stands. Buttons her coat.

PEARL (CONT'D)
Eight o'clock, you said.

ROSALIND
Eight.

PEARL
I'll bring coffee.

She goes. The door sighs closed behind her.

Devante and Rosalind alone. The radio talks to nobody.

He crosses to the counter. Picks up the last trophy. Offers it to her, base first.

She takes it. Wraps it. Her page is today's sports section.

DEVANTE
Which one's this?

ROSALIND
Doubles. Nineteen ninety-one. With Mom.

DEVANTE
Huh.

He sets his hand on the box lid. Leaves it there a moment.

Then he reaches up and kills the radio.

The alley goes quiet. Only the hum of the cooler. Only the paper.
